</p><p>The preoperative fasting time for elective surgeries was shorter than for urgent ones (<xref rid="t3" ref-type="table">Table 3</xref>). This result also appears in the analysis of clear liquids or full meals before surgery (<xref rid="t4" ref-type="table">Table 4</xref>).</p><p>The factors associated with preoperative fasting time that remained in the final linear model were age (p&#x0003c;0.001) and the occurrence of previous surgery (p=0.002), both adjusted for sex and type of surgery (elective or urgent). For each additional year of life, there was a 10-minute increase in fasting time (&#x003b2;=10, 95%CI 5.2-14.8). Previous surgery increased fasting time by 76.6 minutes (&#x003b2;=76.6, 95%CI 28.0-125.1) (<xref rid="t5" ref-type="table">Table 5</xref>).</p><p>
